---
/*
 * The same envelope at two hops. The task list is identical in both; only the
 * current-task index moves, and each service increments it. That is why no
 * service needs to know what comes after it, and why the pipeline is reordered
 * by changing the message rather than the code.
 *
 * Field labels are the descriptions from content-source, not invented JSON keys:
 * the source describes what the envelope carries without naming the keys.
 *
 * Styling comes from the shared .diagram rules in src/styles/global.css.
 */
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="-8 -6 574 288"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="envelope-title envelope-desc"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    <title id="envelope-title">A self-describing message envelope at two pipeline hops</title>
    <desc id="envelope-desc">
      The same envelope is shown twice. Both copies carry an identical ordered task list of extract,
      translate, score and filter, plus per-service configuration, a target queue and exception and
      traceback fields. At the first hop the current index points at extract; after the document
      extraction service increments it, the index points at translate. Only the index changes.
    </desc>

    <!-- Hop 1 -->
    <g class="node">
      <rect x="0" y="18" width="228" height="164" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="0" y="12">hop 1</text>
    </g>
    <g class="field">
      <text x="14" y="42">ordered task list</text>
      <text x="14" y="104">per-service config</text>
      <text x="14" y="126">target queue</text>
      <text x="14" y="148">exception / traceback</text>
    </g>
    <g class="offset">
      <text x="26" y="62">extract, translate,</text>
      <text x="26" y="80">score, filter</text>
    </g>
    <g class="accent">
      <text x="14" y="172">current index -&gt; extract</text>
    </g>

    <!-- The broker carries it from one service to the next. -->
    <g class="collapse">
      <line x1="236" y1="100" x2="292" y2="100" />
      <polygon points="292,94 306,100 292,106" />
      <text x="271" y="89" text-anchor="middle">ActiveMQ</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Hop 2: identical envelope, index advanced by one. -->
    <g class="node">
      <rect x="316" y="18" width="228" height="164" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="316" y="12">hop 2</text>
    </g>
    <g class="field">
      <text x="330" y="42">ordered task list</text>
      <text x="330" y="104">per-service config</text>
      <text x="330" y="126">target queue</text>
      <text x="330" y="148">exception / traceback</text>
    </g>
    <g class="offset">
      <text x="342" y="62">extract, translate,</text>
      <text x="342" y="80">score, filter</text>
    </g>
    <g class="accent">
      <text x="330" y="172">current index -&gt; translate</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Which service is doing the work at each hop. -->
    <g class="node-label">
      <text x="114" y="212" text-anchor="middle">document extraction</text>
      <text x="430" y="212" text-anchor="middle">translation</text>
    </g>
    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="114" y="232" text-anchor="middle">increments the index</text>
      <text x="430" y="232" text-anchor="middle">reads its own next task</text>
      <text x="272" y="270" text-anchor="middle">reorder the pipeline by editing the message</text>
    </g>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Both hops carry the identical ordered task list. Only the current-task index moves, incremented by
    each service as it finishes. A message therefore knows its own remaining itinerary, which means no
    service needs to know what comes after it and the pipeline can be reordered by changing the message
    rather than the code.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
